   "oldText": "For each marker column, process the horizontal-bar rows from top to bottom. At each such row, extend that row's horizontal bar color vertically upward in the marker column, starting at that bar row, until the row above the preceding horizontal-bar row for that column (or to the top edge if there is no preceding bar row). Equivalently, the marked column is partitioned into vertical intervals by the horizontal-bar rows; fill each interval with the color of the horizontal bar at its lower boundary. At an intersection with an existing horizontal bar, keep that bar's own color. This fills the marked-column gaps in the bars and the spaces above them, while leaving the original bottom marker stubs unchanged. The stub's own color is just part of the marker and remains unchanged; it is not the color used for the completed vertical segments.\n\nMarkers may lie below all bars; identify them as monochrome vertical runs near the bottom that designate columns. In the demos, the indicated columns were c3 and c9 (zero-based). The marker colors did not transfer into the added pixels.",
   "newText": "For each marker column, identify the horizontal bars that have a one-cell gap exactly in that column: the bar's same-color cells lie on both sides of the gap (possibly separated by other structure only when visibly indicated). Ignore horizontal bars that simply do not reach the marker column. These gap rows are the vertical-line endpoints. Fill the marker column with the gap-row bar colors, partitioning the column into intervals between consecutive gap rows: from the top edge through the first gap row, use the first gap's bar color; between two gap rows, use the lower gap's color; and from the last gap row downward through the last horizontal-bar level, use that last gap's color. Preserve the original horizontal bar cells and the bottom marker stubs. The stub's own color is just part of the marker and remains unchanged; it is not the color used for the completed vertical segments.\n\nMarkers may lie below all bars; identify them as monochrome vertical runs near the bottom that designate columns. In the demos, the indicated columns were c3 and c9 (zero-based). The marker colors did not transfer into the added pixels."
